WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.599
Billions and billions of galaxies. The

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:13.840
universe is so vast we can't even

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:19.000
imagine what those numbers mean. But 14

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.000
billion years ago, none of it existed

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.800
until the Big Bang.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.480
The Big Bang is the origin of space and

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:33.360
the origin of time itself.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.160
We take a journey through space and time

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.640
from the beginning to the end of the

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.640
universe itself.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.120
Since the late 1920s, everything we know

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:55.520
about how our universe works has been

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.199
turned upside down.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.559
It's important to realize how much our

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:00.559
picture of the universe has changed in

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:02.000
the last century.

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:03.920
At the beginning of the 20th century,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.360
the conventional wisdom in science was

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.119
that the universe was static and

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.119
eternal.

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:13.040
In 1929, that all changed.

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:15.439
At the Mount Wilson Observatory above

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.159
Los Angeles, astronomer Edwin Hubble

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:20.240
discovered galaxies aren't stuck in one

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.400
place.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.720
Not only are they moving, but they're

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.720
flying away from Earth at incredible

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.720
speeds.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.600
This was the first real evidence of the

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.600
Big Bang.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:40.400
All galaxies on average were moving away

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.640
from us. And strangers still, those that

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.280
were twice as far away were moving twice

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.280
as fast, and those that were three times

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.600
as far away were moving three times as

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.759
fast, and so on.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.439
Everything was moving away from us.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:57.680
>> It became known as Hubble's law. His

00:01:55.439 --> 00:02:00.880
discovery is still the starting point

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.840
for exploration of the Big Bang.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.000
>> What Hubble convincingly demonstrated by

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.960
seeing the motion of those galaxies is

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.960
that the universe is expanding.

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:17.959
Theoretically, an expanding universe

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.959
must have started from a single point.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.080
By measuring how fast the universe is

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.800
expanding, astronomers calculated

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.800
backwards and figured out when it burst

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.640
into life.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.160
People ask the question, "How do you

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:36.720
know that the universe is 13.7 billion

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:40.239
years old?" I mean, smarty pants, you

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.840
weren't there 13.7 billion years ago.

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:43.760
Well, when you watch television on

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.840
videotape, you hit the stop button when

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.560
you see an explosion and you could run

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.879
it backwards and see when it actually

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.599
took place. The same thing takes place

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:55.840
with cosmology. We can run the videotape

00:02:53.599 --> 00:03:00.120
backwards and then calculate when it all

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.120
came from a cosmic explosion.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.239
You don't have to be an astronomer to

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:09.239
look back in time.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.239
If you gaze up at the night sky, you're

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.959
seeing stars that are millions of light

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:20.080
years away. Meaning, it took the light

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:23.599
from those stars millions of years to

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.239
get here. So, if you look far enough,

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:29.640
you should be able to see the beginning

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:29.640
of the universe.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.480
Named for the groundbreaking astronomer,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.519
the Hubble Space Telescope allows us to

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.480
look deep into the universe, back in

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:45.280
time, and closer to the moment of the

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.440
Big Bang.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.080
But for scientists, winding back the

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.080
clock to the Big Bang was only the first

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.080
step.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.959
When people first hear about the Big

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.640
Bang theory, they say, "Well, where did

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:02.080
it take place? It took place over there.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.560
It took place over there. Where did it

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.760
take place?" Actually, it took place

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:12.840
everywhere because the universe itself

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.840
was extremely small at that time.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.000
These are only some of the most abstract

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.720
and difficult concepts there are. So,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.040
here's a mind bender. What came before

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.120
the Big Bang? The philosophers in

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.080
ancient times used to say, "How could

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.320
something arise from nothing?" And

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.160
what's amazing to me is that the laws of

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.000
physics allow that to happen. And it

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.680
means that our whole universe,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.600
everything we see, everything that

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.280
matters to us today could have arisen

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.800
out of precisely nothing.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.520
It's one of the biggest hurdles to

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.520
understanding the Big Bang. First, you

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.040
have to buy into the premise that

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.120
something was created out of nothing.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.759
It's impossible to describe the moment

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.919
of creation in human language. All we

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:04.479
know is that from what may have been

00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:07.440
nothing, we go to a state of

00:05:04.479 --> 00:05:11.199
almost infinite density and infinite

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.440
temperature and infinite violence.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:16.400
At the dawn of time, the universe

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:20.000
explodes into existence from absolutely

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:22.400
nothing into everything.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.000
But everything is actually a single

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:30.800
point, infinitely small, unimaginably

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.280
hot, a super dense speck of pure energy.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.639
The Big Bang

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:40.160
was so immense that it brought into

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:42.639
existence all of the mass and all of the

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:45.039
energy contained in all of the 400

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:47.440
billion galaxies we see in our universe

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:49.919
in a region smaller than the size of a

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.520
single atom.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:53.360
The entire observable universe was a

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.440
millionth of a billionth of a centimeter

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.080
across at that time. Everything was

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.520
compressed into incredibly hot, dense

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.160
region.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.360
>> It's not even matter yet, just a point

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:08.800
of raging energy. It was the beginning

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.520
of the universe and everything in it.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.319
>> Everything was simple. All the forces

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.440
that we know about today were one and

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:20.840
the same. The universe was amorphous. It

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.840
had no structure.

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:29.600
>> In that instant of creation, all the

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.160
laws of physics, the very forces that

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.160
engineer our universe, began to take

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.160
shape.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.240
The first force to emerge was gravity.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.160
The fate of the universe, its size,

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:50.520
structure, and everything in it was

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.520
decided in that moment.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.680
Carlos Frank studies how gravity shaped

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.960
the universe by creating artificial

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.960
universes in this supercomput.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.880
He gives each one a different amount of

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.880
gravity.

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:14.960
The first one he tried had too little

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:17.919
resulting in well nothing.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.120
>> Gravity it has shaped our universe. Or

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:23.680
if gravity was weaker than it is we

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.479
would have a very boring universe in

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.960
which everything would be flying apart

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:31.280
so fast that there would be no galaxies

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.680
forming.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.919
Next he programmed a universe with too

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.919
much gravity.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.639
If gravity was stronger than we think it

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.319
is, again, we'll end up with a failed

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:46.560
universe. Everything will end up in

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:49.120
black holes.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.360
It has to be just so. It has to be just

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.080
right.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.520
Lucky for us, the Big Bang got it just

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.840
right. The perfect amount of gravity. In

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.000
the turmoil of forces after gravity

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.639
emerged, still a fraction of a second

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.360
after the Big Bang, a shock wave of

00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:12.680
energy erupted and expanded the universe

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.680
in all directions at incredible speed.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:21.440
All of space expanded by an unbelievably

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:23.919
large factor in a fraction of a second.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.400
We think that in less than a millionth

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:30.400
of a millionth of a millionth of a

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:34.320
millionth of a second, space expanded by

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:38.680
a factor bigger than a million million

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.680
million million times.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.279
And for the record, that's faster than

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:53.880
the speed of light. But wait, doesn't

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:53.880
that break one of the laws of physics?

00:08:55.839 --> 00:08:59.519
>> Even school children know that quote,

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.360
"You can't go faster than the speed of

00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:03.760
light." But actually, there's a loophole

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.440
there. You see, nothing can go faster

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:09.440
than light. Nothing being empty space.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.959
>> Don't worry. This idea gives even the

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.880
best minds in science a headache.

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:16.880
But it's critical to understanding the

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.800
early universe.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.680
Scientists think it took less than a

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.680
millionth of a millionth of a millionth

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.160
of a millionth of a second for the

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.880
universe to expand from the size of an

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.959
atom to a baseball.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.360
That may not sound like much, but it's

00:09:30.959 --> 00:09:36.320
like a golf ball expanding to the size

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:39.040
of the Earth in the same amount of time.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.640
That means it was expanding faster than

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.959
the speed of light. That's fast.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.560
So many things were happening so fast in

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:49.120
the early universe because everything

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.279
was so close together that we needed a

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.320
new unit of time to describe things.

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:57.360
It's called plank time. To understand

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:59.600
just how short a plank time is, consider

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.320
this. There are more units of plank time

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:05.440
in 1 second than all the seconds since

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:07.920
the big bang. The math is mind-blowing.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:10.880
There more than 31 million seconds in a

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.519
year. And it's been 14 billion years

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:16.519
since the Big Bang. So multiply

00:10:13.519 --> 00:10:16.519
31,556,926

00:10:17.839 --> 00:10:22.800
by 14 billion and what you get is a

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:25.519
really big number. It's a time scale

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.160
that's so small that all human intuition

00:10:25.519 --> 00:10:30.560
goes out the window. If we look at our

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:34.160
watches and measure 1 second, we can ask

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:39.279
how many plank times is that? Well, it

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:43.399
is a billion billion billion billion

00:10:39.279 --> 00:10:43.399
billion plank times.

00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:52.640
So now the big bang is only a few plank

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.680
times old. A fraction of a second after

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:59.200
the big bang, the universe is so small

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.600
it can fit in the palm of your hand.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:03.920
But in another tiny fraction of a

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.440
second, it expands to the size of the

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.839
Earth.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.800
Then, moving faster than the speed of

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:13.839
light, it grows larger than our solar

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.519
system. And it's still just a raging

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:19.920
storm of superheated energy.

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:22.320
It'd be hotter and denser and more

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.440
violent than anything that we can

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:28.800
experience in the universe today.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:31.440
Even the interior of a star is calm and

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:34.880
serene by comparison to the violence of

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:36.880
the earliest moments of the Big Bang.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:39.120
Temperatures were so hot that even the

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.200
atoms of your body would disintegrate.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.240
So hot, in fact, that the atoms would be

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:47.360
ripped apart. How hot? Trillions of

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:50.240
degrees hot. But as the universe

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.040
continues to expand, it also begins to

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:57.480
cool. Dropping temperatures trigger the

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.480
next stage in the universe's evolution.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.880
The raw energy of the explosion

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:05.880
transforms into tiny subatomic

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.880
particles.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.240
It's the first matter in the universe.

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.480
This conversion of energy into matter

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:20.800
was predicted by Albert Einstein years

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:24.040
before anyone started talking about the

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.040
big bang.

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:29.519
It's the one scientific equation every

