particles in the universe in the

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:10.760
earliest moments of the Big Bang.

00:15:11.199 --> 00:15:15.440
The extreme temperature of the early

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:20.920
universe energizes the subatomic

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:20.920
particles. They appear. They disappear.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:28.920
They race around at incredible speeds.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.920
It's pure chaos.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:37.600
It's like people. If they're excited and

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:39.279
running around fast to catch trains at a

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.360
train station, they'll be moving around

00:15:39.279 --> 00:15:43.600
quickly,

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:46.079
but eventually they calm down and get

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.079
slower. That's what's been happening to

00:15:46.079 --> 00:15:49.920
our universe in a sense. The particles

00:15:48.079 --> 00:15:52.160
were moving around very fast and as the

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.079
universe cools down, the particles move

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:57.040
more slowly and in some sense less

00:15:54.079 --> 00:15:59.600
randomly.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:04.120
As the universe cools, the particles

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:04.120
stop changing back into energy.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:14.079
Now there are more and more subatomic

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:16.720
particles, but it's still a hot, violent

00:16:14.079 --> 00:16:18.959
place.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.000
All this is happening in fractions of a

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:25.320
second, too small to detect.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:25.320
Looking good.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:33.160
May 2009, the European Space Agency

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:33.160
launches the Planck satellite.

00:16:34.959 --> 00:16:39.279
It orbits the sun, scanning the

00:16:37.279 --> 00:16:41.040
temperature of the entire visible

00:16:39.279 --> 00:16:43.440
universe.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:45.519
It's so sensitive, it can measure the

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.639
temperature of the cosmic microwave

00:16:45.519 --> 00:16:49.920
background to within a millionth of a

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:51.839
degree.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.519
>> The blue spots in the map are cold

00:16:51.839 --> 00:16:56.079
spots. They're going to evolve to become

00:16:53.519 --> 00:16:58.079
large empty voids. The red spots in the

00:16:56.079 --> 00:17:01.040
map, those are hot spots. They're going

00:16:58.079 --> 00:17:03.120
to form clusters of galaxies. This map

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:05.520
is a blueprint for how our universe is

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.199
going to form and evolve.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:08.880
When we look at this map, we're also

00:17:07.199 --> 00:17:11.679
looking back in time and seeing the

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:14.319
echoes of creation.

00:17:11.679 --> 00:17:16.480
The tiny variations measured by plank go

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:19.360
on to form the galaxies that fill the

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:21.520
universe. And inflation explains them

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:24.240
perfectly.

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:26.319
But scientists need a smoking gun,

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:30.120
something out of science fiction,

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:30.120
gravitational waves.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:36.000
>> One of the tests is that we might be

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:37.760
able to see uh the gravity waves that

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:40.160
were produced at the very end of

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:43.120
inflation.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:46.000
Gravitational waves stretch and contract

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:49.039
spaceime itself. They travel through the

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:51.440
universe like ripples in a pond.

00:17:49.039 --> 00:17:54.160
Scientists believe that the violence of

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:56.880
inflation sent these waves reverberating

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:59.280
throughout the cosmos. If we could see

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:01.919
them, it would be case closed.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:04.480
Scientists would know that inflation was

00:18:01.919 --> 00:18:06.880
real.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:09.120
March 2014,

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:13.120
30 years since Alan Guth came up with

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:16.000
inflation, a telescope at the South Pole

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:17.919
shakes the world. Today, scientists

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:20.480
announced they have discovered what was

00:18:17.919 --> 00:18:24.880
going on in the earliest moments of our

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:26.960
universe right after the Big Bang.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:29.840
>> This is the most exciting scientific

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:32.160
result in my career. Inflation was an

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:34.720
incredibly violent process. Different

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:36.400
parts of the universe all expanding at

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.919
somewhat different rates, but all faster

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:39.840
than the speed of light. And this

00:18:37.919 --> 00:18:41.760
difference in expansion rate produced

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:44.080
gravitational waves. And these

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:46.400
gravitational waves produced a signature

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.919
on the microwave sky that we've now

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:49.600
seen.

00:18:47.919 --> 00:18:53.160
>> It's something I am so happy has

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:53.160
happened in my lifetime.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:19:00.000
>> The data is an almost perfect fit for

00:18:57.919 --> 00:19:01.679
inflation. What we're finding is that

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.840
the very simplest models of inflation

00:19:01.679 --> 00:19:06.240
are agreeing beautifully what with what

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:07.919
uh observations are being made and

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.520
that's incredibly gratifying and

00:19:07.919 --> 00:19:13.720
provides I think very strong evidence

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:13.720
that inflation really happened.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:20.799
Inflation explains why galaxies are

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:23.679
spread so smoothly across the cosmos

00:19:20.799 --> 00:19:26.880
in the tiniest fraction of a second. It

00:19:23.679 --> 00:19:32.679
transforms a minute and uniform bit of

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:32.679
space into the entire visible universe.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:40.720
And the implications are stunning. Maybe

00:19:37.919 --> 00:19:44.080
it wasn't just the visible universe that

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:46.480
grew under inflation.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:49.200
Some scientists now believe that other

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:51.679
regions of space too distant for their

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:54.559
light to ever reach us underwent

00:19:51.679 --> 00:19:55.679
inflation too and are still inflating.

00:19:54.559 --> 00:19:56.880
Now

00:19:55.679 --> 00:19:59.600
>> one of the profound things about

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:02.080
inflation is once it starts it's hard to

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:03.840
stop it.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:06.240
>> Inflation never stops everywhere. It

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.960
stops in places and every place where it

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:10.480
stops one produces a universe.

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:12.080
Inflation

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:15.760
seems to go on producing other pocket

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:18.480
universes literally forever.

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:20.400
>> Indeed, inflation makes our big bang and

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:23.919
then goes on and makes lots of other big

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:28.280
bangs and creates this big collection of

00:20:23.919 --> 00:20:28.280
universes, the multiverse.

00:20:28.799 --> 00:20:34.799
>> Our universe may be just one of an

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:37.520
infinite collection of universes in a

00:20:34.799 --> 00:20:40.799
multiverse much much larger than we ever

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:43.039
imagined. And some scientists argue that

00:20:40.799 --> 00:20:46.080
not only are there multiple universes

00:20:43.039 --> 00:20:50.520
out there, they think every possible

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:50.520
universe must exist.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:58.559
People identical to us would live out

00:20:54.720 --> 00:21:02.840
every possible parallel life in every

00:20:58.559 --> 00:21:02.840
possible parallel world.

00:21:03.039 --> 00:21:08.880
A mind-blowing outcome.

00:21:06.799 --> 00:21:11.600
Now that we have such strong evidence

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:15.760
for inflation, it's time to take ideas

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:18.960
like the multiverse really seriously.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:21.039
Let's reset. The universe is less than a

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:24.000
trillionth of a trillionth of a second

00:21:21.039 --> 00:21:26.640
old, and the seeds of its galaxies have

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:31.760
already been sown.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:34.080
But everything is still pure energy.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:38.840
So where does all the matter, the stuff

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:38.840
of stars and you and me come from?

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:46.799
As inflation ends, the brightest flash

00:21:43.919 --> 00:21:48.799
in history floods the cosmos with

00:21:46.799 --> 00:21:50.720
radiation.

00:21:48.799 --> 00:21:53.440
During the first second of the universe,

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:56.080
it was unimaginably hot and dense. It

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:58.080
was basically a ball of energy.

00:21:56.080 --> 00:22:01.919
>> It's not a place you'd want to stand in.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.919
You'd vaporize pretty quickly.

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:05.760
Everything zipping round at the speed of

00:22:04.559 --> 00:22:08.080
light.

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:10.720
>> This universe full of radiation is

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:13.600
nothing like our universe today. Full of

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:16.000
stuff, material stuff.

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:17.840
>> Because it was so hot. Atoms didn't

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:21.039
exist. Matter as we know it didn't

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:23.919
exist. The universe was a dense

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:27.120
soup of radiation.

00:22:23.919 --> 00:22:30.559
>> The earliest universe is a chaos of pure

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:34.760
energy. But how does it transform into a

00:22:30.559 --> 00:22:34.760
universe full of matter?

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:41.200
To answer that question, we have to turn

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:45.559
to Einstein and a very famous equation.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.559
E= MC².

00:22:45.919 --> 00:22:50.720
>> Before Einstein, people said matter is

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:53.200
matter, energy is energy, and never the

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:55.760
twain shall meet. Along comes Einstein

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:59.280
and says not so fast. They really are

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.280
the same thing.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:06.240
Einstein realized that matter is just

00:23:03.039 --> 00:23:08.400
concentrated energy.

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:10.240
This insight transformed our

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:12.720
understanding of the universe and

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:15.360
allowed us to unleash devastating

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:19.039
destruction

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:23.600
and atomic bombs.

00:23:19.039 --> 00:23:26.159
Energy E that can turn into matter and

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:28.559
vice versa.

00:23:26.159 --> 00:23:30.799
Even a small teaspoon of matter would be

00:23:28.559 --> 00:23:34.440
enough to unleash the power of hundreds

00:23:30.799 --> 00:23:34.440
of hydrogen bombs.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:43.600
While atom bombs convert matter into

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:46.799
energy, in the Big Bang, energy starts

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:48.400
to turn into matter.

00:23:46.799 --> 00:23:51.360
But it's nothing like the matter that

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:56.520
makes up the world we live in. So what

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:56.520
does this primal matter look like?

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:05.120
This is Brook Haven National Laboratory

00:24:03.039 --> 00:24:07.600
in Long Island,

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:12.799
home to the relativistic heavy ion

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:15.120
Collider, or Rick for short.

00:24:12.799 --> 00:24:17.520
Here they recreate the matter that

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:19.840
filled the embryionic universe 1

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:20.880
millionth of a second after the Big

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.720
Bang.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:24.480
>> In a sense, uh, Rick, it really is a

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:26.000
time machine. We're reproducing the

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:27.679
conditions that existed in the early

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:31.679
universe on the order of one

00:24:27.679 --> 00:24:35.360
microssecond after the big bang.

00:24:31.679 --> 00:24:38.880
>> Rick fires gold nuclei around a 2 1/2

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:43.720
mile circular tunnel 78,000 times a

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:43.720
second at almost the speed of light.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:51.279
Then it smashes them together in the

00:24:47.919 --> 00:24:53.760
giant star detector.

00:24:51.279 --> 00:24:55.840
Imagine

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:57.760
smashing two cars together in a head-on

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:00.400
collision

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:05.480
and working out what the cars look like

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:05.480
by analyzing the debris thrown off.

00:25:05.679 --> 00:25:10.000
That's what the team at Rick is doing.

00:25:07.919 --> 00:25:15.640
They're hunting for the building blocks

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:15.640
of protons by smashing them to pieces.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.279
Each collision that you see here, you

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:23.600
can see that they're different. Some

00:25:21.279 --> 00:25:26.240
collisions have more tracks coming out

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:27.840
of them. The curved lines represent, in

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:30.159
fact, they are the particles that come

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:32.320
out of the collision. And you can see

00:25:30.159 --> 00:25:33.919
each collision generates a different

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:36.880
number of particles depending on the

00:25:33.919 --> 00:25:39.279
violence of the collision.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:42.880
What Mike and his team see is a spray of

00:25:39.279 --> 00:25:44.720
the most fundamental particles of all,

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:47.520
quarks.

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:50.320
Quarks are normally bound inside protons

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:53.279
and neutrons, but give quarks enough

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:55.840
energy and they break free.

00:25:53.279 --> 00:25:57.919
>> We expected these quarks once they're

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.760
liberated from the protons and neutrons

00:25:57.919 --> 00:26:02.240
not to interact much with each other

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:05.200
just to stream out into our detector.

00:26:02.240 --> 00:26:07.600
What we found was the most perfect fluid

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:10.080
that was ever discovered, ever measured

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:13.039
uh on on the planet. So it actually

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:15.679
flows much more easily than water does.

00:26:13.039 --> 00:26:18.400
This was absolutely surprising.

00:26:15.679 --> 00:26:21.919
>> And Rick shows us that the perfect fluid

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:24.799
of elementary particles occurs at just 1

00:26:21.919 --> 00:26:27.440
millionth of a second into the Big Bang.

00:26:24.799 --> 00:26:30.000
But like everything in the first second,

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:33.200
it doesn't last for long.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.760
As the universe expands, it cools and

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:38.080
something called the strong force makes

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:39.919
its presence felt.

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:42.240
The most important characteristic of the

00:26:39.919 --> 00:26:44.799
strong force is the confinement of

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:47.760
quarks inside protons and neutrons that

00:26:44.799 --> 00:26:49.600
we see us today.

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:52.960
When the temperature of the universe

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:55.120
drops to two trillion degrees, the

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:58.400
strong force clumps quarks together in

00:26:55.120 --> 00:27:01.760
groups of three, making protons and

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:04.640
neutrons, the building blocks of atoms.

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:07.880
The universe begins to take the form we

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.880
know today.

00:27:08.159 --> 00:27:12.640
But as matter is bursting into existence

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:15.919
all across the universe, there's still

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:22.360
something missing.

00:27:15.919 --> 00:27:22.360
Somehow this matter has no mass.

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:32.600
The matter in today's universe has mass.

00:27:28.159 --> 00:27:32.600
On Earth, we feel it as weight.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:40.520
In space, objects can feel weightless,

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:40.520
but they still have mass.

00:27:40.559 --> 00:27:46.760
>> If this astronaut had no mass, he'd be

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:46.760
in big trouble.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:52.240
>> You can't actually slow something down

00:27:50.799 --> 00:27:53.840
if it doesn't have any mass. If

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:56.320
something doesn't have any mass, it has

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:58.480
to move at the speed of light. There's

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:00.399
no way of stopping it. So a universe

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:02.559
that's full of matter but matter which

00:28:00.399 --> 00:28:05.360
has no mass is one in which it just

00:28:02.559 --> 00:28:08.000
looks like a big ball of light.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:10.240
>> The early universe had no mass, just

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:12.480
elementary particles fizzing at the

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:14.880
speed of light.

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:17.520
But today our universe is full of

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:21.679
planets and stars that clearly have

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:24.080
mass. So where does this mass come from?

00:28:21.679 --> 00:28:26.240
Apparently, there's some field that

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:28.159
permeates the entire universe and

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:30.080
different elementary particles will

00:28:28.159 --> 00:28:31.440
interact with it in different ways. And

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:33.440
it's the interaction of the particle

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:36.240
with the field that gives the particle

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:38.799
its property that we call mass.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:40.960
Scientists call it the Higs field after

00:28:38.799 --> 00:28:44.240
Peter Higgs, one of the first scientists

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:46.960
to propose it in the 1960s. The Higs

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:49.440
field is invisible. It stretches

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:51.520
throughout space and is accompanied by a

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:54.159
fundamental particle called the Higs

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:56.640
bosen which interacts with particles of

00:28:54.159 --> 00:28:59.520
ordinary matter as they pass through the

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:03.760
field. The more an object interacts with

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:05.760
the Higs field, the more mass it gains.

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:09.840
Without the Higs field and the Higs

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:12.720
bosen, there is no mass.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:15.279
The Higs field is a beautiful idea, but

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:17.679
does it really exist? There's only one

00:29:15.279 --> 00:29:21.200
place to find out at the biggest and

00:29:17.679 --> 00:29:26.120
most advanced machine ever built. The

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:26.120
Large Hadron Collider at CERN.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:31.919
>> Joe Incadella hopes to prove the field

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:32.559
exists by smashing the Higs Bosen out of

00:29:31.919 --> 00:29:33.919
the shadows.

00:29:32.559 --> 00:29:35.200
>> So, I actually need all of you,

00:29:33.919 --> 00:29:36.640
including the cameraman, to go through

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:40.000
when the other door is open. Just go all

