singularities exist. They're a very

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:28.079
straightforward prediction of general

00:43:25.280 --> 00:43:29.839
relativity. That doesn't fit well with

00:43:28.079 --> 00:43:31.599
what quantum mechanics says. Quantum

00:43:29.839 --> 00:43:33.760
mechanics tends to fuzz things out. It

00:43:31.599 --> 00:43:36.079
doesn't really like singularities.

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:38.800
>> In some theories of quantum mechanics,

00:43:36.079 --> 00:43:41.920
the science of the small, there's a

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:43.760
limit to how small you can go.

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:45.520
For example, I'm not going to be able to

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:47.520
fold up this paper more than seven

00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:49.119
times.

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:50.720
One,

00:43:49.119 --> 00:43:52.319
two,

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:56.640
three,

00:43:52.319 --> 00:43:58.800
four. I'm feeling good. Five. Oh, man.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:44:00.800
Six. Can I do it? Can I do it? Can I

00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:02.400
break the laws of physics? No, I can't.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:06.000
There's a limit. I just can't go past

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:08.480
it. You can't keep folding paper or

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:11.359
space into smaller and smaller

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:13.359
fragments. And according to most laws of

00:44:11.359 --> 00:44:17.200
quantum mechanics, you can't have

00:44:13.359 --> 00:44:19.760
anything infinitely small and infinitely

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:23.280
dense.

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:26.880
Singularities seem to be doomed. So, is

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:29.040
the Big Bang story wrong or are we just

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:32.240
too dumb to work it out?

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:36.960
>> Our laws of physics are our best

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:40.240
attempts to model with mathematics all

00:44:36.960 --> 00:44:42.960
of our observations of the universe.

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:45.200
The universe doesn't care what we think.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:47.280
The universe doesn't care how we

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:50.960
understand it. This is just our attempts

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:53.359
to explain the behavior that we see. And

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:57.839
the earliest moments of the Big Bang is

00:44:53.359 --> 00:45:01.240
a big example of where our understanding

00:44:57.839 --> 00:45:01.240
falls short.

00:45:03.520 --> 00:45:08.800
Maybe the answer lies in a combination

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:10.160
of general relativity and quantum

00:45:08.800 --> 00:45:13.880
mechanics.

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:13.880
>> But they won't play ball.

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:25.599
>> Imagine the rules of quantum mechanics

00:45:22.640 --> 00:45:28.400
are like the rules that lacrosse players

00:45:25.599 --> 00:45:30.480
might use to play their game. And the

00:45:28.400 --> 00:45:32.880
rules of general relativity are the

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:35.520
rules that baseball players would use to

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:37.680
play their game. If you're just watching

00:45:35.520 --> 00:45:39.760
a baseball game, then they're just

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:41.520
following the rules of baseball or

00:45:39.760 --> 00:45:43.200
general relativity. If you're just

00:45:41.520 --> 00:45:45.920
watching a lacrosse game, you're

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:47.599
watching the rules of lacrosse play out.

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:49.760
You're watching the rules of quantum

00:45:47.599 --> 00:45:52.160
mechanics. But if you take one team from

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:54.640
lacrosse and one team from baseball and

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:56.800
put them together and ask them to start

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:58.640
playing, they don't even know how to

00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:01.280
interact with each other. It's

00:45:58.640 --> 00:46:03.599
fundamentally different rules that

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:05.839
simply don't connect.

00:46:03.599 --> 00:46:07.359
Each of these pillars of modern science,

00:46:05.839 --> 00:46:10.960
quantum mechanics and general

00:46:07.359 --> 00:46:14.480
relativity, are wonderful in their

00:46:10.960 --> 00:46:16.640
domain. But when we try to marry them,

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:18.880
which is what we need to do to describe

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:20.960
the earliest moments of the universe, it

00:46:18.880 --> 00:46:23.040
all goes haywire.

00:46:20.960 --> 00:46:25.520
>> Perhaps both teams playing with a

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:28.640
unified set of rules will shed some

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:30.560
light on the Big Bang.

00:46:28.640 --> 00:46:33.040
>> Merging together quantum mechanics and

00:46:30.560 --> 00:46:34.560
general relativity is the gold standard.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:36.160
It's what every theoretical physicist

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:38.160
would really love to do in the modern

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:40.240
age. We haven't done it yet. We have

00:46:38.160 --> 00:46:42.079
ideas. So when I say we haven't done it

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:43.280
yet, we don't agree on what the right

00:46:42.079 --> 00:46:46.079
idea is.

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:49.359
>> What will ultimately reconcile quantum

00:46:46.079 --> 00:46:52.800
mechanics with gravity? Is there even a

00:46:49.359 --> 00:46:55.839
reconciliation between them? What do we

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:59.040
need? Do we need more surprising, more

00:46:55.839 --> 00:47:02.160
powerful observations, new data that we

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:05.680
weren't expecting another genius or a

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:07.839
thousand geniuses to come along and find

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:12.680
the route through the mathematics to

00:47:07.839 --> 00:47:12.680
marry it? It's probably all the above.

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:19.680
>> So, a major premise of the Big Bang

00:47:16.000 --> 00:47:23.280
story remains unproven. But what about

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:25.760
Monsenior Lmetre's other assertions?

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:28.800
Was the infant universe, the primeval

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:32.160
atom, intensely hot?

00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:34.720
And if so, just how hot was it? The Big

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:37.760
Bang story claims this point was

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:39.839
infinitely small.

00:47:37.760 --> 00:47:41.839
But scientists have not been able to

00:47:39.839 --> 00:47:43.440
prove the existence of such

00:47:41.839 --> 00:47:46.160
singularities.

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:48.880
In our universe, we see all galaxies

00:47:46.160 --> 00:47:52.319
receding from all other galaxies on

00:47:48.880 --> 00:47:55.599
average. Imagine if you were looking at

00:47:52.319 --> 00:47:57.440
trains leaving a station. If you ran the

00:47:55.599 --> 00:48:00.240
clock backwards, the trains would

00:47:57.440 --> 00:48:03.040
converge to the same station. Now, did

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:05.440
these trains come from the same station?

00:48:03.040 --> 00:48:08.720
Probably. Did they come from the exact

00:48:05.440 --> 00:48:10.560
same platform? Probably not. You can't

00:48:08.720 --> 00:48:12.960
fit all the trains onto the same

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:15.920
platform. But while physicists can't

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:18.400
prove everything came from an infinitely

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:20.960
small and dense singularity, they're

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:24.000
convinced the observable universe did

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:26.480
expand from one small point. And this

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:28.800
point was incredibly dense and

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:30.400
incredibly hot.

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:32.800
Imagine that you and a bunch of friends

00:48:30.400 --> 00:48:34.960
are in a very large room and you're all

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:36.880
hanging out and it all seems normal. But

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:39.599
now you're all crammed together in a

00:48:36.880 --> 00:48:40.960
very small elevator and it starts to

00:48:39.599 --> 00:48:42.720
feel much warmer because you're

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:44.160
interchanging all this heat. It's sort

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:45.839
of like that in the early universe.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:48.880
Everything is smashed together.

00:48:45.839 --> 00:48:50.960
Everything is very hot.

00:48:48.880 --> 00:48:53.520
It makes sense theoretically that this

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:55.839
period was intensely hot. But how do you

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:57.680
prove it?

00:48:55.839 --> 00:49:02.319
How do you take the temperature of the

00:48:57.680 --> 00:49:04.319
early universe which began 13.8 billion

00:49:02.319 --> 00:49:06.400
years ago?

00:49:04.319 --> 00:49:09.200
You can't. But you can take the

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:10.559
temperature of the coldest part of the

00:49:09.200 --> 00:49:12.800
universe. Now,

00:49:10.559 --> 00:49:14.240
>> so if you go away from all the stars and

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:16.559
get away from all the galaxies, you

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:18.640
might think space was infinitely cold,

00:49:16.559 --> 00:49:22.000
absolute zero. But it turns out it's

00:49:18.640 --> 00:49:24.640
not. Empty space has a temperature of

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:29.839
roughly 455°

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:33.440
F below zero, 5° higher than absolute

00:49:29.839 --> 00:49:36.160
zero. Where did these mysterious extra

00:49:33.440 --> 00:49:38.240
5° come from?

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:39.839
Big bang believers thought they had the

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:42.400
answer.

00:49:39.839 --> 00:49:45.119
They claimed this faint trace of heat

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:48.480
was left over from the incredibly hot

00:49:45.119 --> 00:49:52.480
infant universe. Getting the proof took

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:55.119
decades, but it came in 1964

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:56.960
by pure accident.

00:49:55.119 --> 00:49:58.480
Pensius and Wilson were Bell Labs

00:49:56.960 --> 00:50:01.599
engineers and they were given an

00:49:58.480 --> 00:50:04.160
assignment to measure certain radio

00:50:01.599 --> 00:50:06.079
signals for the idea of sending wireless

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.440
signals via telephone so rural areas

00:50:06.079 --> 00:50:10.559
could have telephone.

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:12.880
>> They used a radio antenna shaped like a

00:50:10.559 --> 00:50:15.280
giant horn. The problem was no matter

00:50:12.880 --> 00:50:17.359
where they pointed this horn, they kept

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:20.720
hearing kind of a static just a radio

00:50:17.359 --> 00:50:22.960
noise coming from every direction.

00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:25.200
and they thought, "Okay, maybe it's a a

00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:27.280
satellite." But it didn't match up with

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:29.520
any satellite positions. There was a

00:50:27.280 --> 00:50:30.960
nearby army base and they called up the

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:32.400
army base said, "Hey, are you

00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:34.720
broadcasting at this frequency?" And

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:36.960
they said, "No, we're not." Thought

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:38.640
maybe it's pigeon poop.

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:40.880
>> Well, you know, there are these pigeons

00:50:38.640 --> 00:50:43.520
nesting inside the antenna, and they're

00:50:40.880 --> 00:50:44.880
droppings are creating this noise in

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:46.640
your telescope. So, they actually went

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:48.559
inside and scraped out all these pigeon

00:50:46.640 --> 00:50:51.040
droppings, but no matter what they did,

00:50:48.559 --> 00:50:53.520
the noise remained. They tried

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:55.119
everything they could to remove this

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:57.760
background noise and they finally

00:50:55.119 --> 00:51:00.000
realized it was coming from the sky. It

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:02.000
was real.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.680
What they were hearing was not radio

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:08.960
waves but a different form of radiation.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:12.680
Microwaves, a heat signature left over

00:51:08.960 --> 00:51:12.680
from the Big Bang.

00:51:13.359 --> 00:51:20.400
They had discovered the cosmic microwave

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:22.640
background, a ghostly snapshot of the

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:24.480
early universe.

00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:27.280
Different colors highlight subtle

00:51:24.480 --> 00:51:29.680
variations in temperature.

00:51:27.280 --> 00:51:33.119
The cooler blue areas will develop to

00:51:29.680 --> 00:51:35.920
form stars and galaxies.

00:51:33.119 --> 00:51:38.720
The warmer orange areas will eventually

00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:40.160
make up the vastness of intergalactic

00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:42.800
space.

00:51:40.160 --> 00:51:46.160
The cosmic microwave background is a

00:51:42.800 --> 00:51:49.760
literal baby picture of our universe.

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:52.720
It's the equivalent of a picture of you

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:54.720
when you were 7 seconds old.

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:57.440
>> We can date the cosmic microwave

00:51:54.720 --> 00:52:00.400
background to 380,000

00:51:57.440 --> 00:52:02.720
years after the Big Bang. The

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:06.079
temperature here is estimated to be

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:10.240
5,000° F.

00:52:06.079 --> 00:52:12.880
But how hot was the big bang? As we run

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:18.359
the clock backwards, the universe gets

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:18.359
smaller and the temperature increases.

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:21.839
We know what the temperature of the

00:52:20.079 --> 00:52:23.440
cosak microwave background was. But

00:52:21.839 --> 00:52:25.119
prior to that time, we know the universe

00:52:23.440 --> 00:52:26.960
was getting smaller and smaller and

00:52:25.119 --> 00:52:30.160
smaller, and therefore it had to get

00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:33.839
hotter and hotter and hotter. But can we

00:52:30.160 --> 00:52:35.599
find out how hot?

00:52:33.839 --> 00:52:38.319
In the early universe, it was much

00:52:35.599 --> 00:52:40.880
smaller, denser, and hotter than it is

00:52:38.319 --> 00:52:44.720
today. And in fact, it was so hot, it

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:47.200
could fuse hydrogen into helium.

00:52:44.720 --> 00:52:49.200
25% of the mass in the early universe is

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:51.359
fused into helium in this time scale of

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:53.119
just a few minutes.

00:52:51.359 --> 00:52:54.720
So, it's trillions and trillions of

00:52:53.119 --> 00:52:58.520
times more than the amount of fusion

00:52:54.720 --> 00:52:58.520
that's going on in the sun.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:07.040
Extremely high temperatures are required

00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:10.559
to fuse hydrogen into helium. Scientists

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:13.359
estimate fusion started 100 seconds

00:53:10.559 --> 00:53:18.000
after the Big Bang when temperatures

00:53:13.359 --> 00:53:20.960
reached 1 billion° F. During the very

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:23.760
first fractions of the very first second

00:53:20.960 --> 00:53:26.880
of the Big Bang, some estimate the

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:29.920
temperature could have reached 250

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:32.480
million trillion trillion degrees

00:53:29.920 --> 00:53:34.880
Fahrenheit. But what sparked this

00:53:32.480 --> 00:53:38.000
massive release of energy here at the

00:53:34.880 --> 00:53:42.000
birth of the Big Bang?

00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:44.800
The initial moments of our universe are

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:47.040
a source of frustration because it'd be

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:49.280
really nice to know that, but also a

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:51.599
source of curiosity. This is the

00:53:49.280 --> 00:53:54.240
frontiers of physics. This is where

00:53:51.599 --> 00:53:56.720
we're really pushing things to try to

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:58.640
understand the fundamental aspects of

00:53:56.720 --> 00:54:00.800
reality.

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:04.000
But even if we can back up the Big Bang

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:06.559
story by proving the way everything came

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:09.599
from a tiny hot point, there's still

00:54:06.559 --> 00:54:13.520
another problem. Where did everything

00:54:09.599 --> 00:54:14.960
that made up that tiny point come from?

00:54:13.520 --> 00:54:17.440
You can't get something from nothing,

00:54:14.960 --> 00:54:19.520
right? We all know that. Except it looks

00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:21.200
like we got everything from nothing. The

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:23.680
entire universe seems to have appeared

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:25.760
out of nowhere. How can that work?

00:54:23.680 --> 00:54:29.200
>> This is one of the big open questions in

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:32.400
cosmology. um the origin of the universe

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:34.240
and I think that people have variously

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:35.040
said stuff like oh the universe has come

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:39.040
from nothing

00:54:35.040 --> 00:54:43.119
>> like tada now there's a universe but

00:54:39.040 --> 00:54:45.599
remember we don't have data about the

00:54:43.119 --> 00:54:50.359
earliest moments of the universe we

00:54:45.599 --> 00:54:50.359
don't know what was going on

00:54:50.559 --> 00:54:55.680
struggling in the dark to get insight

00:54:53.200 --> 00:54:56.559
into what may have happened before the

00:54:55.680 --> 00:54:59.520
big bang

00:54:56.559 --> 00:55:03.599
that period of apparent nothingness,

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:07.040
physicists look to empty space. But does

00:55:03.599 --> 00:55:08.960
empty really mean there's absolutely

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:10.400
nothing there?

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:13.040
>> It's not that there's something coming

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:15.680
from nothing? Because that old-fashioned

00:55:13.040 --> 00:55:18.559
idea of nothing just doesn't apply to

00:55:15.680 --> 00:55:22.240
what we think of as empty space.

00:55:18.559 --> 00:55:25.599
>> It turns out empty space is far from

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:28.240
empty. The vacuum of space is really a

00:55:25.599 --> 00:55:30.559
writhing sea, a wash with charged

00:55:28.240 --> 00:55:32.240
quantum particles and electromagnetic

00:55:30.559 --> 00:55:34.559
fields.

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:36.720
The vacuum of space itself, it can be a

00:55:34.559 --> 00:55:38.480
very dynamic thing. Matter can

00:55:36.720 --> 00:55:40.800
spontaneously appear out of the vacuum

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:43.040
and then spontaneously annihilate.

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:45.520
>> The vacuum is full of particles and

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:47.040
antiparticles that are whizzing into

00:55:45.520 --> 00:55:49.200
existence and then disappearing,

00:55:47.040 --> 00:55:51.680
colliding with each other.

00:55:49.200 --> 00:55:54.640
Space is full of virtual particles

00:55:51.680 --> 00:55:56.079
popping in and out of existence.

00:55:54.640 --> 00:55:58.160
>> And there's no question that they're

00:55:56.079 --> 00:56:02.119
real. Their effects are absolutely

00:55:58.160 --> 00:56:02.119
visible. We can see them.

00:56:03.200 --> 00:56:09.200
>> In extreme physics, things can get

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:12.440
strange. Maybe nothing is something

00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:12.440
after all.

00:56:12.880 --> 00:56:18.240
If empty space contains particles that

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:20.559
apparently come from nothing, could some

00:56:18.240 --> 00:56:23.920
sort of similar process have triggered

00:56:20.559 --> 00:56:29.119
the big bang?

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:30.799
The quantum vacuum itself can randomly,

00:56:29.119 --> 00:56:33.760
spontaneously,

00:56:30.799 --> 00:56:36.960
without any input, just

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:39.520
have a lot of energy, perhaps enough

00:56:36.960 --> 00:56:42.640
energy to spark something that we would

00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:42.640
call a big bang.

00:56:44.319 --> 00:56:49.680
There are many speculative theories

00:56:46.319 --> 00:56:52.480
about the origin of the universe. But is

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:55.280
its sudden appearance out of nothingness

00:56:52.480 --> 00:56:58.559
the only trick it pulled off?

00:56:55.280 --> 00:57:01.440
It also made matter from energy. The

00:56:58.559 --> 00:57:04.319
universe is full of galaxies, stars,

00:57:01.440 --> 00:57:06.640
planets, and comets. Where did they all

00:57:04.319 --> 00:57:10.880
come from? According to the Big Bang

00:57:06.640 --> 00:57:12.640
narrative, from one tiny dot. When the

00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:14.640
universe began, there was actually no

00:57:12.640 --> 00:57:16.799
room for matter at all. The temperature

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:19.760
was so high, the spaces were so

00:57:16.799 --> 00:57:21.839
compressed that matter couldn't exist.

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:25.799
So, how did the universe manage to

00:57:21.839 --> 00:57:25.799
become full of matter?

00:57:25.839 --> 00:57:31.520
In the primeval atom, there was no room

00:57:28.480 --> 00:57:33.280
for matter, but it was crammed full of

00:57:31.520 --> 00:57:36.400
energy.

00:57:33.280 --> 00:57:40.799
And as Einstein tells us, all we need to

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:45.280
create matter is energy. According

00:57:40.799 --> 00:57:48.280
to E= MC², energy and matter are

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:48.280
interchangeable.

00:57:48.559 --> 00:57:54.720
Einstein taught us with special

00:57:51.040 --> 00:57:57.920
relativity that energy and matter are

00:57:54.720 --> 00:58:00.720
two sides of the same coin. You can

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:03.359
convert matter into energy by say

00:58:00.720 --> 00:58:05.280
blowing something up.

00:58:03.359 --> 00:58:08.240
The most fearsome example of converting

00:58:05.280 --> 00:58:12.040
matter into energy was the atomic bomb

00:58:08.240 --> 00:58:12.040
developed in the 1940s.

00:58:15.119 --> 00:58:24.160
But with the big bang, this process was

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:27.839
reversed. Energy created matter.

00:58:24.160 --> 00:58:31.319
Matter that expanded out and out to fill

00:58:27.839 --> 00:58:31.319
a whole universe.

00:58:32.160 --> 00:58:37.680
And in cosmic terms, the universe, where

00:58:35.040 --> 00:58:38.960
matter would develop, grew remarkably

00:58:37.680 --> 00:58:40.960
fast.

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:43.520
>> Right after the universe was born, it

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:45.680
had an unbelievable growth spurt.

00:58:43.520 --> 00:58:47.839
Basically going from being a toddler to

00:58:45.680 --> 00:58:52.079
a teenager in the single tick of a

00:58:47.839 --> 00:58:54.960
clock. How could the universe get so big

00:58:52.079 --> 00:58:57.520
so quickly? If we believe the big bang

00:58:54.960 --> 00:59:02.079
story, it appears to have broken one of

00:58:57.520 --> 00:59:05.359
the most fundamental laws of physics.

00:59:02.079 --> 00:59:10.119
Did the early universe really grow

00:59:05.359 --> 00:59:10.119
faster than the speed of light?

00:59:13.200 --> 00:59:17.760
In the classic big bang story, the

00:59:15.280 --> 00:59:21.200
observable universe expands from a ball

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:24.559
of energy smaller than an atom.

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:28.319
Today the universe is estimated to be 93

00:59:24.559 --> 00:59:30.160
billion lightyear in diameter.

00:59:28.319 --> 00:59:32.160
>> Think about the vast universe that we

00:59:30.160 --> 00:59:34.640
see all around us today. And it was once

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:36.319
a tiny tiny little volume unimaginably

00:59:34.640 --> 00:59:40.839
dense. The universe must have gone

00:59:36.319 --> 00:59:40.839
through a colossal growth spurt.

00:59:41.839 --> 00:59:45.920
>> With a steady rate of expansion, there

00:59:44.000 --> 00:59:50.520
simply hasn't been enough time for the

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:50.520
universe to grow to its current size.

00:59:51.200 --> 00:59:55.680
Regions of the universe that were close

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:58.079
neighbors in the past are now so far

00:59:55.680 --> 01:00:02.680
apart that their separation can't be

00:59:58.079 --> 01:00:02.680
explained by normal expansion.

01:00:03.520 --> 01:00:09.119
And the huge size of the cosmos is not

01:00:06.559 --> 01:00:12.160
the only strange thing we've discovered.

01:00:09.119 --> 01:00:17.680
The universe is what astronomers call

01:00:12.160 --> 01:00:20.720
flat. Our universe is lumpy and bumpy at

01:00:17.680 --> 01:00:23.359
small scales. There's galaxies, there's

01:00:20.720 --> 01:00:26.799
black holes, there's people, there's all

01:00:23.359 --> 01:00:30.319
sorts of junk. But at large scales,

01:00:26.799 --> 01:00:35.319
global scales, truly universal scales,

01:00:30.319 --> 01:00:35.319
our universe is flat.

01:00:35.839 --> 01:00:44.559
Could this universal flatness and the

01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:44.559
super rapid growth somehow be connected?

01:00:46.799 --> 01:00:51.920
We know that the big bang wasn't an

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:56.079
explosion or matter in the universe

01:00:51.920 --> 01:00:59.920
would be unevenly distributed.

01:00:56.079 --> 01:01:01.839
Yet something pushed everything outwards

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:04.960
and fast.

01:01:01.839 --> 01:01:08.240
But what?

01:01:04.960 --> 01:01:10.480
In 1980, Alan Guth, a young Stanford

01:01:08.240 --> 01:01:14.799
cosmologist, came up with a potential

01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:18.000
answer. His theory of inflation says the

01:01:14.799 --> 01:01:20.480
observable universe expanded from being

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:22.000
smaller than an atom to the size of a

01:01:20.480 --> 01:01:24.799
basketball

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:27.119
almost instantaneously

01:01:24.799 --> 01:01:28.880
in this tiny fraction of a second at the

01:01:27.119 --> 01:01:30.960
beginning of the universe. like a

01:01:28.880 --> 01:01:32.720
millionth of a second, but a millionth

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:34.880
of that, a millionth of that, and a

01:01:32.720 --> 01:01:37.119
millionth of that. The universe expanded

01:01:34.880 --> 01:01:39.760
by take the size of the universe at that

01:01:37.119 --> 01:01:41.920
time and multiply it by a one with say

01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:44.880
roughly 50 zeros behind it.

01:01:41.920 --> 01:01:46.960
>> Inflation drove

01:01:44.880 --> 01:01:49.119
the accelerated expansion of the

01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:51.599
universe. Made it get really, really big

01:01:49.119 --> 01:01:54.000
really, really fast. And then it

01:01:51.599 --> 01:01:56.720
stopped.

01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:58.319
>> Inflation seems to solve two big bang

01:01:56.720 --> 01:02:01.599
headaches.

01:01:58.319 --> 01:02:05.680
how the universe got so huge so quickly

01:02:01.599 --> 01:02:08.559
and why it's so flat.

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:10.640
Because it inflated everywhere at once,

01:02:08.559 --> 01:02:13.440
all the energy in the universe which

01:02:10.640 --> 01:02:18.319
would turn into matter was pushed out

01:02:13.440 --> 01:02:20.480
evenly at the same time and same pace.

01:02:18.319 --> 01:02:24.079
And the first stars grow together to

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:27.440
become the first galaxies. Even here, us

01:02:24.079 --> 01:02:28.960
in the solar system, we're born. were

01:02:27.440 --> 01:02:33.720
seated

01:02:28.960 --> 01:02:33.720
in the event of inflation.

01:02:34.400 --> 01:02:39.520
>> Parts of the universe that are now

01:02:36.079 --> 01:02:43.680
separated by 93 billion lightyear once

01:02:39.520 --> 01:02:46.799
had the same cosmic zip code

01:02:43.680 --> 01:02:48.960
to have expanded so quickly. Inflation

01:02:46.799 --> 01:02:51.520
must have broken one of the fundamental

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:53.520
rules of physics.

01:02:51.520 --> 01:02:55.680
We all know that one rule the universe

01:02:53.520 --> 01:02:57.280
sticks to all the time is that nothing

01:02:55.680 --> 01:02:59.440
can travel faster than the speed of

01:02:57.280 --> 01:03:01.280
light. So how can we possibly say that

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:03.280
the universe expanded faster than the

01:03:01.280 --> 01:03:06.400
speed of light?

01:03:03.280 --> 01:03:08.319
But inflation says the universe expands

01:03:06.400 --> 01:03:11.200
into nothingness.

01:03:08.319 --> 01:03:14.480
There was no outside of the universe.

01:03:11.200 --> 01:03:19.280
The universe was everything.

01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:22.400
So space itself was inflating and space

01:03:19.280 --> 01:03:23.760
can move as fast as it wants. You can't

01:03:22.400 --> 01:03:26.160
go faster than the speed of light

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:28.079
through space, but space itself is

01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:30.559
allowed to stretch and expand as fast as

01:03:28.079 --> 01:03:33.520
it wants. And that's what our universe

01:03:30.559 --> 01:03:35.359
is doing. The idea of inflation smooths

01:03:33.520 --> 01:03:37.039
out the universe in kind of a peculiar

01:03:35.359 --> 01:03:39.200
way. You can kind of think of it as

01:03:37.039 --> 01:03:40.799
having a a sheet with a lot of wrinkles

01:03:39.200 --> 01:03:43.200
in it. And if you take that sheet and

01:03:40.799 --> 01:03:44.559
snap it really hard, those wrinkles very

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:48.640
suddenly flatten out.

01:03:44.559 --> 01:03:50.799
>> The theory of inflation is one of the

01:03:48.640 --> 01:03:53.440
craziest sounding ideas in the history

01:03:50.799 --> 01:03:56.000
of science. So crazy that it might just

01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:57.839
be right.

01:03:56.000 --> 01:04:01.520
>> Inflation helps us to understand the

01:03:57.839 --> 01:04:04.640
inexplicable. But there's a problem. We

01:04:01.520 --> 01:04:06.240
don't know what triggered or powered

01:04:04.640 --> 01:04:09.119
inflation.

01:04:06.240 --> 01:04:11.119
The inflationary universe idea imagines

01:04:09.119 --> 01:04:14.000
that the universe was suffused with some

01:04:11.119 --> 01:04:15.520
kind of ultra dense energy at early

01:04:14.000 --> 01:04:16.960
times. Something that pushed the

01:04:15.520 --> 01:04:19.280
universe apart

01:04:16.960 --> 01:04:22.160
>> what caused there to be that hot

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:23.680
expanding stuff.

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:26.480
We have to be humble and acknowledge

01:04:23.680 --> 01:04:29.200
that we don't know for sure.

01:04:26.480 --> 01:04:31.680
>> But whatever started it, inflation was

01:04:29.200 --> 01:04:35.280
over in a split second.

01:04:31.680 --> 01:04:37.599
>> It didn't last very long. And this is

01:04:35.280 --> 01:04:41.280
difficult to understand. How did

01:04:37.599 --> 01:04:44.480
inflation stop?

01:04:41.280 --> 01:04:46.960
We don't know. We got none, folks. Well,

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:50.720
we got some things, but

01:04:46.960 --> 01:04:53.359
it's tough. It's tough.

01:04:50.720 --> 01:04:56.000
But has the process of inflation really

01:04:53.359 --> 01:04:58.960
stopped?

01:04:56.000 --> 01:05:01.520
One radical theory proposes if the force

01:04:58.960 --> 01:05:04.640
called inflation kickstarted the

01:05:01.520 --> 01:05:10.039
expansion of one universe, then why not

01:05:04.640 --> 01:05:10.039
another and another and another?

01:05:10.079 --> 01:05:15.440
Is inflation creating a whole series of

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:19.079
new universes?

01:05:15.440 --> 01:05:19.079
Is there a multiverse?

